##
    Directives that cannot do what they were written to do.

    Every one of these is reported, and not one of them stops the build. The single error below
    is what rejects this file, and it is here on purpose: it is the thing a directive tried to
    silence and could not.
##

##
    An identifier no diagnostic carries. Almost always a typo, since the alternative is having
    invented one.
##
# ignore PC9999

##
    Not a directive at all. Nothing is recognized until 'warning', 'opinion', or something
    shaped like an identifier follows, so this is a remark and draws nothing — which is the
    point: a comment beginning with the word must stay a comment.
##
# ignore whatever you like

shared model Program

    function Main()
        ##
            A real identifier, suppressible, and nothing this reaches reports it. The line is
            dead weight, which is worth hearing about while it is still easy to remove.

            Only naming an identifier draws this. 'ignore opinion' claims nothing is there, so
            it stays silent with nothing to silence.
        ##
        # ignore PC0403
        Console.WriteLine("nothing here is unreachable");

        ##
            An error cannot be silenced, ever. Without this being said, a reader would write
            the line, meet the error anyway, and conclude the whole mechanism is broken.
        ##
        # ignore PC0300
        integer wrong = "not a number";

        Console.WriteLine(wrong);
    end function

end model
